index 617099f8fa9b85f9e2c71a204d281647684310fd..d6c300858dc407b31b58822649e9f0b4ef739f9c 100644
--- a/docs/reference/frame-rate.md
+++ b/docs/reference/frame-rate.md
@@ -109,10 +109,17 @@ Frame pacing: the host's frame step, engine frame limiters, present intervals an
 - **Symptom:** after every pause (the game out of focus, then back), fullscreen switch or covered window, a game that held 60 fps plays at 20 and then 30 fps for about a second; the log shows `frame step: hold 1 → 3 vsyncs at present … (3 late frames; cost 3.00, GPU 0.89; …)` right after `oe focus state → 1` or `front end: window visible`, then a trial to 2 and a probe back to 1 that holds.
 - **Cause:** `nvn_fstep_next` took the frames after the stall as the scene's cost. The frame that spans the stall is as long as the stall, and the next few are late for the host's reasons: the GPU clock fell while nothing was drawn (the first frame back took 26–42 ms of GPU time after pauses of 1.2–5.8 s and 67 ms after one of 35 s, where the scene takes 15), and the display link is finding its ticks again (acquire and fence waits of 30–160 ms). Three of them in a row stepped up to their cost, 3 vsyncs. After a fullscreen switch the display link's silence (200–570 ms, [Present thread rescues a silent display link](#present-thread-rescues-a-silent-display-link)) did the same (`hold 2 → 3 … cost 6.41, GPU 1.12`).
 - **Fix:** a frame shown for `FSTEP_STALL` (8) vsyncs or more, 133 ms, is a stall: more than twice the longest hold, so no step up would have made it fit. It does not count toward a step up, and neither do the `FSTEP_GRACE` (30) frames after it, as after a probe; a stall inside that grace starts it again. A scene that really is heavier after the stall (a load into a heavy area) steps up 33 frames after it instead of 3. Long frames below a stall (three of 83 ms in a row) step up as before. The 30-second line counts them (`… failed trials), N stalls`).
-- **Not covered:** the first step of a fullscreen switch, `hold 1 → 2` on three mildly late frames (cost 1.3) while the window animates, before any frame is a stall: 30 frames at 30 fps, then the probe holds.
-- **Check:** `make -C dm/gfx/tests GAME=010015100B514000 t_fstep && dm/gfx/tests/t_fstep`: a pause and three late frames, a fullscreen switch with two stalls, 22 ms frames after a stall (step up after grace + 3), 83 ms frames (not stalls), and the same at 120 fps. The pause and fullscreen cases step to hold 3 without the rule.
+- **The host names them too:** `mtl_fstep_settle` (`nvn_mtl.m`, asked once a frame by `fstep_tick`) reports that the game came back into focus (`dm_focus_set(1)`: a pause ended, the window became key) or that the layer's drawable changed size (a resize, entering or leaving fullscreen), and sets `nvn_fstep.settle`: that frame is a stall whatever it took. This covers the first step of a fullscreen switch, `hold 1 → 2` on three mildly late frames (cost 1.3) while the window animates, before any frame is 133 ms long. The time the game was behind in a frame the host names is dropped, not paid back: a pause is not time the game owes, and paid back it played the first quarter second after every pause at double speed (`15 vsyncs paid back`). A stall the host does not name (a load) is paid back up to `FSTEP_DEBT_MAX` as before.
+- **Check:** `make -C dm/gfx/tests GAME=010015100B514000 t_fstep && dm/gfx/tests/t_fstep`: a pause and three late frames, a fullscreen switch with two stalls, 22 ms frames after a stall (step up after grace + 3), 83 ms frames (not stalls), and the same at 120 fps. The pause and fullscreen cases step to hold 3 without the rule. A pause the host names pays nothing back where an unnamed stall pays 15 vsyncs; 40 frames at 1.34 vsyncs around a named window change keep hold 1 and step up without it.
 - **Found on:** [The Legend of Zelda: Ocarina of Time](../../dm/games/0400486024DEC000/README.md) (Mac M4 Pro, 24 GB, a 15-minute session with 12 pauses and 4 fullscreen switches: 543 frames held at 3 vsyncs, nearly all after them). Modelled in `t_fstep`; not yet run on a device with the rule.
 
+### The first frame after a pause waits for the GPU
+
+- **Symptom:** after a pause of about 2 s or more, one frame of 130–300 ms, then the game runs as before; the long-frame line shows the time inside the residency set commit with the submit thread off the CPU (`submit 152.9 ms wall / 9.6 CPU …, off CPU 143.3; residency commits 143.4 ms`) and a first GPU time well above the scene's (26–67 ms for 15). Pauses of 1.2–1.9 s show neither.
+- **Cause (not proven):** with nothing submitted for about 2 s the GPU powers down, and the first call that needs it waits for its return. The commit is where the submit thread meets it; it takes 0.3 ms otherwise (276 commits in 82 ms).
+- **Fix:** `DM_NVN_PAUSE_WARM` (`pause_warm_start` in `nvn_mtl.m`, Mac only): while no frame has been presented for 0.4 s, a 64-byte buffer fill twice a second keeps the GPU powered, for up to 2 minutes of a pause; it does nothing while frames are presented. `0` turns it off.
+- **Found on:** [The Legend of Zelda: Ocarina of Time](../../dm/games/0400486024DEC000/README.md) (Mac M4 Pro: pauses of 2.5, 5.8 and 35 s, 126–178 ms in the commit).
+
 ### A derived profile drops the frame step its build lacks
 
 - **Symptom:** a game whose profile has a frame step (`FRAME_STEP` in its `patches.py`) runs at double speed when packed from another update; the log has no `frame step in whole vsyncs` line, and the frame counter shows 60.
